# Active Exsolved Metal–Oxide Interfaces in Porous Single‐Crystalline Ceria Monoliths for Efficient and Durable CH<sub>4</sub>/CO<sub>2</sub> Reforming

## abstract
Dry reforming of CH4 /CO2 provides an attractive route to convert greenhouse gas into syngas; however, the resistance to sintering and coking of catalyst remains a fundamental challenge at high operation temperatures. Here we create active and durable metal-oxide interfaces in porous single-crystalline (PSC) CeO2 monoliths with in situ exsolved single-crystalline (SC) Ni particles and show efficient dry reforming of CH4 /CO2 at temperatures as low as 450 °C. We show the excellent and durable performance with ≈20 % of CH4 conversion and ≈30 % of CO2 conversion even in a continuous operation of 240 hours. The well-defined active metal-oxide interfaces, created by exsolving SC Ni nanoparticles from PSC Nix Ce1-x O2 to anchor them on PSC CeO2 scaffolds, prevent nanoparticle sintering and enhance the coking resistance due to the stronger metal-support interactions. Our work would enable an industrially and economically viable path for carbon reclamation, and the technique of creating active and durable metal-oxide interfaces in PSC monoliths could lead to stable catalyst designs for many challenging reactions.

Active Exsolved Metal–Oxide Interfaces in Porous Single-Crystalline Ceria Monoliths for Efficient and Durable $\mathbf{CH_{4}}/\mathbf{CO_{2}}$ Reforming
Yongchun Xiao and Kui Xie\*
## Introduction
With the increasing concerns over global warming and climate change, strategies for greenhouse gas reduction have drawn great research attention.[1] $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ are the most contributing gases to greenhouse effect and are both common components of natural and land-fill gas with the fact that they are cheap and abundant, which brings great technological incentives for their efficient utilization.[2] Conversion of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ to syngas could not only contribute to the mitigation of harmful environmental effects of anthropogenic greenhouse gas emissions but also provide energy fuel or feedstock in the Fischer–Tropsch synthesis or methanol synthesis in chemical industry.[3,4]
Dry reforming of methane (DRM) provides a promising method to utilize $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ for the syngas production of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ and CO and it has attracted widespread attention due to its environmental benefits.[5] Although the DRM shows the advantages in the environmental and economic aspects, its industrial applications still faces key challenges due to the deactivation of metal catalyst typically like the conventional Ni-based catalysts suffering from carbon coking during the reaction.[6] Carbon coking may not only cause the catalyst deactivation by blocking active sites, but also fracture catalyst granules or cause a high-pressure drop in the reactor.[8] The agglomeration and sintering of the metal nanoparticles at the surface of support remains another challenge leading to catalytic performance degradation during the DRM reaction.[7] In this case, the growth of nanoparticles on supports due to the weak metal–oxide interactions would lead to the reducing of active sites and hence the performance degradation. Therefore, it is a key challenge to develop highly active and stable catalysts with high resistance to thermal sintering and coking for dry reforming of $\mathrm{CH_{4}/C O_{2}}$ .[9]
A rational design of the DRM catalysts is therefore required to achieve these important breakthroughs regarding the catalyst activity and stability.[10] We however generally face huge challenges in precisely controlling the active sites on the surface and interface of bulk catalysts which is mainly due to that the composition and geometry of the active sites would be changed during the dry reforming process.[11] Therefore, the huge gap between surface/interface science and actual operation conditions is difficult to bridge in the DRM reactions. It is generally accepted that the limited availability and high prices of DRM catalysts based on noble metals (Pt, Pd, Rh, Ru), although highly active for this reaction, have restricted their large-scale applications in industry.[12,13] Low-cost Ni catalyst dispersed on oxide supports could overcome the above limitations and have received appreciable attention.[14] However, Ni-based catalysts could be problematic especially owing to the high temperature of operation or rapid deactivation due to the chemical poisoning (C or O deposition) and nanoparticle sintering at high temperatures.
Substantial efforts have been made in developing highly coke-resistant Ni-based DRM catalysts in the past decades.
Oxides such as $\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}$ , $\mathbf{MgO}$ and $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ are normally used as the supports for loading nanoparticles to improve the durability of catalysts in the DRM reactions.[15] It is generally demonstrated that basic or reducible catalyst supports could facilitate the adsorption and the dissociation of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ gas. Fluorite $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ draws much attentions due to its good dispersion effect for metal nanoparticles and excellent redox performance in different chemical environments versus temperatures.[16] However, the precise control of surface oxygen vacancies, uneven size and sintering of the nano-sized $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ powders are still the remaining essential problems in the DRM reaction.[17]
PSC $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ monoliths combine the advantages of the ordered lattice structures and the disordered interconnected pores, which would have higher thermal stability similar to bulk single crystals and higher catalytic activity similar to nanocrystals in the DRM reactions.[23] The interconnection of the internal pores of the PSC structure facilitates gas diffusion to enhance coking resistance. The exsolution process is an effective method to create strong metal–oxide interfaces by anchoring active metal nanoparticles onto the surface of the oxide supports, by means of which the catalysts would show excellent carbon coking resistance and sintering resistance.[25–28] Specifically, in this process, catalytically active and reducible metal nanoparticles in situ exsolve on the oxide surface from the cations doped into the support oxide in a reducing atmosphere.[29–30] Exsolving Ni from fluorite-type solid solutions of PSC $\mathrm{Ni}_{x}\mathrm{Ce}_{1-x}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ monoliths would yield SC
Ni nanoparticles uniformly anchored onto the surface of PSC $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ monoliths, which would create the well-defined interface structures with strong interaction in the porous architectures. The synergistic activation of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ at the active exsolved metal–oxide interfaces would facilitate the dry reforming reaction and show potential carbon coking resistance and sintering resistance at reduced temperatures.
## Results and Discussion
Here we grow the PSC $\mathrm{Ni}_{x}\mathrm{Ce}_{1-x}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ monoliths at $2\mathrm{cm}$ scale using lattice reconstruction strategy and then in situ exsolve the SC Ni nanoparticles to create well-defined metal–oxide interfaces in the PSC monoliths to enhance the activity and stability of the PSC monoliths for the DRM reactions at temperatures as low as $450^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ We show the significantly enhanced performance of the DRM reactions with the PSC monoliths and the remarkably improved coking and sintering resistance of SC Ni nanoparticles even in a continuous operation of 240 hours.
We grow and cut Ni-doped $\mathrm{CeBr}_{3}$ single crystal into substrates with a diameter of $2\mathrm{cm}$ and then transform them into PSC $\mathrm{Ni}_{x}\mathrm{Ce}_{1-x}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ with the identical dimensions in $99\%\mathrm{Ar}/$ $1\%\mathrm{O}_{2}$ atmosphere at $620–720^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ using lattice reconstruction strategy. The $\mathrm{CeBr}_{3}$ and $\mathrm{NiBr}_{2}$ are pretty sensitive to oxygen and water, which therefore requires that we treat them in glove box with $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ atmosphere. The PSC $\mathrm{Ni}_{x}\mathrm{Ce}_{1-x}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ shows $\approx68\%$ porosity in the porous architectures. Figure 1 a shows the XRD patterns of PSC Ni-doped $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ monoliths with an optical image in the inset with the facets of (200) and (220), respectively, which are grown from the Ni-doped $\mathrm{CeBr}_{3}$ single crystals with the facet of (11-20) and (0002), respectively. The peak of the (200) plane of PSC $\mathrm{Ce}_{1-x}\mathrm{Ni}_{x}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ shifts slightly to higher angle (from $33.360^{\circ}$ to $33.396^{\circ}$ ) in comparison with pure PSC $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ in Figure S1, indicating nominal variation in lattice constant upon doping with $\mathrm{\DeltaNi}$ ions. The lattice constants are found to be 5.3674 and $5.3618\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ for PSC $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ and PSC $\mathrm{Ce}_{1-x}\mathrm{Ni}_{x}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , respectively. And the volume of the unit cell is obtained as 158.042 and $157.981\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{3}$ for PSC $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ and PSC $\mathrm{Ce}_{1-x}\mathrm{Ni}_{x}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , respectively. The above analysis thus confirms that Ni ions occupy the Ce site in the lattice in the form of dopant, leading to a solid solution without observing the formation of impurity phase like NiO. Figure S2 and Figure S3 show the schematic of the crystal growth from Ni-doped $\mathrm{CeBr}_{3}$ to Ni-doped $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ . The lattice mismatch between Ni-doped $\mathrm{CeBr}_{3}$ with the facet of (11-20) and (0002)
and Ni-doped $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ with the facet of (200) and (220) is as low as $\approx1\mathrm{-}8\%$ , which is favorable for the growth of PSC $\mathrm{Ni}_{x}\mathrm{Ce}_{1-x}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . Figure $1\mathsf{b{-c}}$ and Figure S4 show the microstructures of PSC $\mathrm{Ni}_{x}\mathrm{Ce}_{1-x}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ with the facet of (200) and (220), which indicates the homogeneous distribution of mesopores at $\approx25~\mathrm{nm}$ . Figure 1d and Figure S5 show the Cscorrected transmission electron microscopy (Cs-STEM) image of PSC $\mathrm{Ni}_{x}\mathrm{Ce}_{1-x}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , which confirms the 3D interconnected mesopores at $\approx15\mathrm{-}$ $25\mathrm{nm}$ in the PSC architecture. The corresponding SAED pattern shown in the inset reveals that it is a typical cubicfluorite single crystal structure even with the doping of different amounts of Ni in lattice.
Figure 1e and Figure S5 show Cscorrected HRTEM images of porous $\mathrm{Ni}_{x}\mathrm{Ce}_{1-x}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ skeleton with simulated atom stacking in the inset. The elemental mapping image in Figure 1 f shows that Ce, O and Ni are evenly distributed in the skeleton, indicating the homogeneous solid solution without any observable Ni segregation. In this case, the Ni atoms partially occupy the Ce sites in lattice in the form of dopant leading to a homogeneous solid solution. Porous $\mathrm{Ni}_{x}\mathrm{Ce}_{1-x}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ shows the presence of Ce, Ni and O at top layer in the low-energy ion scattering (LEIS) spectra in Figure $1\mathrm{g}$ . In the solid solution, the Ce mainly occurs as $+4$ with slight content of $+3$ at surface and the Ni is in the oxidation state of $+2$ as shown in $\mathbf{\boldsymbol{X}}$ -ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) in Figure h1. The ultra-high energy resolution electron energy loss spectroscopy (EELS) acquired from STEM in Figure S6 has also confirmed the presence of nickel in the PSC $\mathrm{Ni}_{x}\mathrm{Ce}_{1-x}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ (200) and (220) monoliths. Figure 1 i shows the simulated electronic structure of PSC $\mathrm{Ni}_{x}\mathrm{Ce}_{1-x}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ (200) which indicates that the Ni atoms transfer more charge to $\mathrm{~o~}$ atoms in contrast to the Ce-O local structures at $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ surfaces. The $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ temperature programmed reduction $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{TPR}\right)$ ) profiles in Figure S7 show that the PSC $\mathrm{Ni}_{x}\mathrm{Ce}_{1-x}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ contain strong reduction features at around $400^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ mainly due to the exsolution of nickel from lattice and the peaks above $750^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ are mianly attributed to the reduction of bulk ceria. In contrast, the PSC $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ present broad surface reduction features below $550^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and strong bulk reduction peak is also observed above $750^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .
We exsolve SC Ni nanoparticles from the backbone of PSC $\mathrm{Ni}_{x}\mathrm{Ce}_{1-x}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ in reducing atmospheres at the temperature below $500^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Thus, we anchor the SC Ni nanoparticles on PSC $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ and create active metal–oxide interfaces with strong interface interaction in PSC monoliths. Figure 2a shows the surface morphology and XRD pattern of PSC $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ (200) with anchored SC Ni nanoparticles, which demonstrates the in situ exsolved Ni nanoparticles with average size of about $4\mathrm{nm}$ are uniformlly distributed at the surface of skeleton. Figure $2\ensuremath{\mathrm{b}}$ shows the STEM-EDS elemental mapping image of Ni and Ce in the PSC monoliths, indicating that the exsolved Ni particles are evenly embedded into the surface of PSC $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ skeleton. The SEM-EDS maping images in a larger scale are also conducted in Figure S8 and S9, which validates the uniformly distribution of SC Ni nanoparticles. Figure 2c shows the LEIS spectra of PSC $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ , indicating the exsolution of Ni from lattice. Figure 2d and Figure S10 show the $\mathrm{Cs}$ -STEM images of exsolved SC Ni nanoparticles anchored on PSC $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ PSC with the facet of (200) and (220), respectively, which further confirms the SC Ni nanoparticles deeply embeded in backbone. In Figure 2e, the CsHRTEM image gives clear structure information at the metal–oxide interface, which indicates that the exsolved SC Ni nanoparticles embed in PSC $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ to form the heterojunction interfaces between the two phases. Part of the spherical Ni single crystal particle with facet of (111) is epitaxially exsolved out of $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ ( 111), while the other is still embedded in the PSC ${\mathrm{CeO}}_{2}$ substrate. The corresponding ball-and-stick model of the $\mathrm{Ni-CeO}_{2}$ interface in Figure 2 f illustrates the metal-support interaction and the orientation relationship of the Ni nanocrystal and PSC $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ . The cubic structure of Ni matches well to the cubic fluorite structure of $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ with $3.7\%$ mismatch of lattice for $[-111]_{\mathrm{Ni}}//[-111]_{\mathrm{CeO_{2}}}$ and $5\%$ mismatch of lattice for $[-111]_{\mathrm{Ni}}//[-111]_{\mathrm{CeO_{2}}}$ . The good anchoring of SC Ni particles onto the ceria oxide support suggests not only potential catalytically active sites, but also strong interfacial bond, which would be conducive to longterm thermal and chemical stability. The XPS spectra of $\mathrm{Ce}3\mathrm{d}$ and $\mathrm{Ni}~2\mathrm{p}$ in $\mathrm{Ni}_{x}\mathrm{Ce}_{1-x}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ PSC before and after reduction are shown in Figure $2\mathrm{g}$ , which demonstrates that the $\mathrm{Ni^{2+}}$ in $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ lattice is reduced to $\mathbf{N}\mathbf{\dot{i}}^{0}$ and part of $\mathrm{Ce^{4+}}$ is reduced to $\mathrm{Ce}^{3+}$ at the surface during the exsolution process. The electron energy loss line scan spectroscopy (EELS) of Ni and $\mathrm{Ce}$ as shown in Figure $2\mathrm{h}$ , acquired from the position of red dotted line in Figure 2 e, also reveals the SC Ni nanoparticle is partially embedded into the cerium oxide surface. The size of the exsolved Ni nanoparticle size is mainly at $\approx4~\mathrm{{nm}}$ as demonstrated in Figure 2i. The specific surface areas and the dominant pore size are $\approx27{\mathrm{m}}^{2}{\mathrm{g}}^{-1}$ and $\approx25\:\mathrm{nm}$ , respectively, as validated in the BET test in Figure S11.
In situ growth of metal–oxide interface architectures not only avoids the agglomeration of nanoparticles at high temperatures, but also create catalytically active sites. The SEM and AFM images in Figure 3a–b indicate that the exsolved SC Ni nanoparticles are homogeneously anchored on the surface of PSC $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ monoliths. Figure 3 c indicates that the height of the exsolved SC Ni nanoparticle is $\approx1.1\ \mathrm{nm}$ at the substrate surface. The fact that the SC Ni nanoparticles are indeed universally socketed at the surface of PSC $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ monoliths, which is confirmed by etching them with concentrated acid. The left behind pits with similar number density as the nanoparticles themselves are validated in Figure 3d–e. The AFM line scan detail of an etched surface (taken from
Figure 3 e) is shown in Figure 3 f, demonstrating that the SC Ni nanoparticles are indeed embedded to a considerable depth of $\approx1.38\:\mathrm{nm}$ in the PSC support. The pit size distribution based on the counting of 300 pits is presented in Figure $3\mathrm{g}$ , which follows the Gaussian distribution and the average size is similar to the results of SC Ni nanoparticles at $\approx4~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . The 3D AFM image of PSC $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ with corrosion pits is shown in Figure $3\mathrm{h}$ , and the corresponding surface roughness profile is presented in Figure 3 i. The typical feature of the surface morphology of PSC $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ after acid etching treatment is clearly observed and the surface roughness is around $\pm2.11\mathrm{nm}$ .
We control the density of in situ grown interface architectures for the fact that it determines the number of active sites and directly affects catalytic performance. The density of SC Ni nanoparticles and the length of $\mathbf{Ni-CeO}_{2}$ interface at the surface of the PSC $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ monolith with exsolved SC Ni nanoparticles increase linearly with the Ni weight percentage varying from $0.71\%$ to $2.31\%$ as detected by ICP and EDS in Figure S13,S14. Figure 4 e shows the surface oxygen exchange coefficient $\left(K_{\mathrm{ex}}\right)$ versus the Ni amount of the PSC $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ monolith with exsolved SC Ni nanoparticles. The structure of exsolved Ni anchoring on PSC $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ monoliths enhances the surface oxygen exchange coefficient by up to $\approx10$ times in contrast to pure PSC $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ monoliths, again indicating that the anchoring of Ni on $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ not only enables the metal–oxide interface to effectively activate the $\mathrm{\bfC-H}$ bond but also facilitates the adsorption and dissociation of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ at active interfaces. And the surface oxygen exchange coefficient of the single crystals is higher than that of the polycrystalline $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ system, indicating the effectiveness of the well-defined surface structure of single crystal to improve catalytic activity.[35] We believe that the metal–oxide interface interaction is the reason why the surface oxygen exchange coefficient of the PSC $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ monolith with exsolved SC Ni nanoparticles is enhanced by $\approx10$ times in contrast to PSC $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ monolith.
Figure $5\mathrm{a-c}$ show the DRM performance over the PSC $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ (200) monoliths with different contents of exsolved SC Ni nanoparticles with the feeding gas of $10\%\mathrm{CO}_{2}/10\%\mathrm{CH}_{4}/$ $80\%$ Ar at $450^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The conversion of $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ increases linearly versus the contents of SC Ni nanoparticles anchored on the surface of ceria oxide, indicating the catalytic activity is effectively improved by creating the active metal–oxide interfaces in PSC architectures. We also observe the significantly enhanced DRM performance at elevated temperature.
Figure $5\mathrm{g}$ shows the correlation between the content exsolved SC Ni nanoparticles, the length of metal–oxide interface, surface oxygen exchange coefficient, $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ conversion, which indicates a strong linear dependence of catalytic performance on the length of active metal–oxide interfaces. Actually, the porous $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ single crystals have the unique feature of bulk porous structure, which shows the long-range ordering of lattice and disordering of interconnected pores in a porous singlecrystalline architecture. The well-defined surface of skeleton in the porous $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ single crystals is in a high-energy state with clear step atoms, which is not a fixed facet in traditional concept. Thus, the inner surface of the porous $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ single crystal we are discussing here is actually a continuous high-energy distorted surface composed of various lattice orientations. The porous single crystals with different crystal planes have similar pore structures as shown in Figure S8, which therefore leads to the negligible difference of the catalytic performance between PSC $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ (200) and PSC $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ (220) monoliths with in situ exsolved single-crystalline Ni nanoparticles as shown in Figure 5 and Figure S15.
After the continuous operation of 240 hours, we observe that the SC Ni nanoparticles anchored on the surface of
PSC $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ monoliths show the generally identical size of $\approx4~\mathrm{{nm}}$ as before the DRM reaction, as shown in Figure $5\mathrm{h}$ , which further validates the excellent thermal stability of our exsolved metal–oxide interface in the PSC monoliths. And the single-crystalline structure of the PSC $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ monolith remains unchanged after the test shown in the XRD pattern in the inset. In Figure 5i, the Temperature Programmed Oxidation (TPO) profile after the durability test shows the negligible coke after the DRM test, indicating the enhanced coking resistance of the catalysts. We further conduct Raman spectroscopy test to validate the coke formation after the durability test in Figure S16a, and we confirm that no carbon signal is observed at the surface of catalyst, indicating the effectiveness of coking resistance during the DRM at $450^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ We further conduct the TGA test of catalyst after durability test in Figure S16b, and we show the negligible weight loss of the catalyst, again proving the effective resistance to coking. In order to verify and compare the advantages of the exsolved metal–oxide interfaces, we use ALD method to deposit Ni nanoparticles on the surface of the PSC $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ monoliths for comparison. In Figure S17, we could find that the weak metal–oxide interface leads to a slight agglomeration of Ni nanoparticles during the DRM performance even within 30 hours. In contrast, the size of exsolved SC Ni nanoparticles remains unchanged even after the durability test for 240 hours.
## Conclusion
In conclusion, we have created active metal–oxide interfaces in PSC $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ monoliths by in situ exsolving singlecrystalline Ni nanoparticles to enhance the catalytic activity and durability in the DRM performance. The well-defined active interface structures are favorable to significantly enhance the interface interaction in the PSC $\mathrm{CeO}_{2}$ monoliths, which would prevent metal nanoparticle aggregation to enhance coking resistance. We show efficient $\mathrm{CH_{4}/C O_{2}}$ reforming at a reduced temperature as low as $450^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with excellent durability even after a continuous operation of 240 hours. The current work would open a novel path to the rational design of efficient and durable catalytic materials not only for the DRM reactions but also in many other challenging areas.
